From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 00:40:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] virtio/gpu: resize displays at runtime

Add krun_display_resize(ctx, display, width, height, width_mm, height_mm),
so a host window can drive the guest display size. Before krun_start_enter
it edits the configuration; afterwards (any thread) it queues the change on
a DisplayControl that krun_start_enter registers per context (under the
CTX_MAP lock, so a context is always in one of the two maps) and shares
with the virtio-gpu device.

The GPU worker owns the display list: a request writes the control queue's
eventfd, and the worker applies every queued resize before processing
commands (a burst collapses into one notification), sets
VIRTIO_GPU_EVENT_DISPLAY in events_read and raises a configuration change
interrupt. Linux then fetches GET_EDID + GET_DISPLAY_INFO again and sends a
DRM hotplug event. events_clear writes are handled; a display event raised
after the driver's last events_read survives its clear (the newer
interrupt re-runs the driver's handler, which must still see it).

The generated EDID follows the new size (preferred detailed timing at the
configured refresh, the given physical size). Large modes that overflow the
16-bit pixel clock with the default blanking now get CVT reduced blanking,
and the clock is clamped instead of wrapping; the screen size in cm no
longer wraps either. Sizes the detailed timing cannot describe (> 4095) are
rejected.

Scanouts are untouched: the guest keeps its old framebuffer until it sets
one of the new size, which reconfigures the display backend with the new
display size through the existing path.
